      "id": 1,
      "text": "Barnesville was founded in 1826 and named for Gideon Barnes, a local tavern proprietor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barnesville%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town grew from a trading post and stagecoach stop on routes between Macon and Columbus, becoming a rail stop in 1841.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Barnesville,_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Every September, the town celebrates its heritage with an annual Buggy Days festival featuring vintage carriages and local crafts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barnesville%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Gordon State College, a public university founded in 1872, sits in Barnesville and drives much of the local economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
